      "id": 1,
      "text": "Challenge was once the terminus of a narrow‑gauge logging railroad that hauled timber to the Beanville mill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Challenge%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Andrew Martin Leach built seven sawmills and a 50‑mile wooden flume that could move lumber at up to 50 mph.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/challenge_california",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name came from a wager between rival sawmill operators over timber‑cutting efficiency.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/challenge_california",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Challenge sits on a ridge that separates the North Fork Yuba River canyon from the South Fork Feather River canyon, dropping 1,500 to 2,000 feet in a few miles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/challenge_california",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A post office opened in 1895, and the town still has a small school that serves six local towns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Challenge%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Mediterranean climate delivers about 45 inches of rain each year, mostly from November to April, supporting conifer forests but limiting dry‑summer farming.",
